## Why This Is Asked
This question targets practical API testing of asynchronous flows, idempotency, and webhook reliability—common in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys for POST safety
- Async polling and state progression
- Webhook delivery and retry/backoff
- Negative paths and concurrency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for idempotency
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const key = 'idemp-123';
  const a = await post('/orders', {items:[1,2]}, {headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  const b = await post('/orders', {items:[1,2]}, {headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  assert(a.status===202 && b.status===202);
  assert(a.body.order_id===b.body.order_id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate webhook retries and idempotent processing across failures?
- What tooling would you use to mock downstream services and observe end-to-end retry behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end testing of a distributed transaction orchestrator, focusing on idempotency, compensation, and eventual consistency—critical in payments-like flows.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Distributed orchestration and compensating actions
- Partial-failure handling and rollback paths
- End-to-end vs contract tests with downstream services
- Observability and deterministic test data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test skeleton for Node.js (mocha)
describe('POST /payments idempotent flow', () => {
  it('preserves a single transaction for repeated calls with same idempotency key', async () => {
    const key = 'idem-123';
    await postPayment({amount: 100}, key);
    await postPayment({amount: 100}, key);
    const txs = await listPaymentsByKey(key);
    assert.strictEqual(txs.length, 1);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate a bank service outage during the transaction and verify idempotence?
- How would you validate that compensation runs only when partial failure occurs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests must validate correctness and resilience in distributed, event-driven APIs. This question probes end-to-end reliability, idempotency, replay protection, and multi-region consistency under failures.

## Key Concepts

- Outbox pattern and event-bus propagation
- Idempotency keys and replay protection
- Out-of-order and duplicate event handling
- Partial regional failures and graceful degradation
- Eventually consistency SLAs and monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton using Node.js and supertest
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../src/app');

describe('Publish idempotency', () => {
  it('replays with same Idempotency-Key should not duplicate writes', async () => {
    const id = 'feed-42';
    const key = 'idem-42';
    await request(app).post(`/feeds/${id}/publish`).set('Idempotency-Key', key).send({ content: 'hello' });
    await request(app).post(`/feeds/${id}/publish`).set('Idempotency-Key', key).send({ content: 'hello' });
    // verify single write via read API or DB count
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model latency and jitter in the event bus during tests, and what are acceptable SLA thresholds?
- How would you extend tests to verify exactly-once delivery in the presence of network partitions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for high-concurrency, real-time messaging with ordering and durability are common in collaboration apps. This question probes ability to design end-to-end tests that cover connection scalability, in-order delivery, offline replay, backpressure, and resilience to broker outages.

## Key Concepts

- WebSocket load testing and client choreography
- In-order delivery guarantees with per-room sequencing
- Durable stores for replay (Redis Streams, Kafka, or similar)
- Backpressure handling and reconnection logic
- Failure modes: partial broker outages, network partitions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for sequencing validation
let roomId = 'room-42';
let lastSeq = 0;
ws.onmessage = (evt) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(evt.data);
  if (msg.roomId === roomId) {
    if (msg.seq <= lastSeq) fail('out-of-order');
    lastSeq = msg.seq;
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure end-to-end latency under peak load and under broker outage?
- What tooling would you use to generate 10k concurrent connections and validate ordering?
